    "page_id": "topic-10-7-4",
    "page_path": "/explore/society/regional-and-community-studies/social-structures-of-border-provinces/",
    "name": "Social Structures of Border Provinces",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/society/regional-and-community-studies/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s border societies are shaped by relationships among local livelihoods, transport networks, state regulation, and movement across international boundaries. Historical research in the east and studies of Kurdish border labor reveal different regional experiences that cannot be reduced to one frontier model.",
    "tags": ["Borderlands", "Regional society", "Cross-border labor", "Citizenship", "Trade networks"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:45:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:45:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Border Provinces: Social Networks, Mobility and Livelihoods",
      "description": "Examine eastern borderland history and Kurdish cross-border labor, with clear distinctions between regional evidence and broader interpretation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "borderland-perspective",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A boundary is also a social setting",
        "content": "A border province is an administrative territory; a borderland is a social space shaped by movement and relationships across a boundary. The distinction helps explain why national maps alone cannot describe local livelihoods. Historical research on Iran’s eastern frontier and interview-based work on Kurdish cross-border labor examine different combinations of transport, employment, state power, and personal risk. They support comparison, but not a single account of every Iranian frontier. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "eastern-networks",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Eastern towns grew through connections",
        "content": "Mikiya Koyagi’s study of the eastern borderlands examines the decades following Reza Khan’s rise to power. Dozdab, now Zahedan, developed as a transfer point linking railway traffic from British India with motor and camel transport inside Iran. Connections extended toward Quetta, the Makran coast, Kerman, and towns farther north. This history places an apparently peripheral town within a wider commercial network. [s1]\n\nTransport work involved people with different backgrounds, including Baluch and Hazara camel operators and Sikh truck drivers from India. The network was not simply a local community facing an external state. Koyagi shows how attempts to regulate movement introduced constraints while also encouraging new ways of moving goods and people across borders. His evidence comes primarily from British consular reports, whose institutional perspective must remain visible when interpreting local experience. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "authority-and-belonging",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Citizenship and regulation had practical consequences",
        "content": "Koyagi’s analysis treats tribal departures and smuggling as connected problems of sovereignty. State authorities sought to determine who could move, what could circulate, and which claims of belonging they would recognize. Borderland networks could frustrate these ambitions even as roads and other infrastructure expanded official reach. [s1]\n\nThe broader lesson is analytical rather than a claim about present law: infrastructure can increase both state capacity and the opportunities for cross-border exchange. A road or railway does not have only one social effect. The historical account also cautions against assuming that all activities later classified as illicit began outside established regional economies. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "kurdish-border-labor",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Labor and vulnerability on the western frontier",
        "content": "In Kurdish border areas, *kolberi* refers to carrying goods across the frontier on one’s back. Research by Sanan Moradi and colleagues examines this demanding work along Iran’s borders with Iraq and Turkey. Its published abstract and accessible introductory material describe exposure to violence, including shootings by border guards, and connect workers’ accounts to scarce employment opportunities. [s2]\n\nThe authors interpret these conditions through a framework of economic disinvestment and securitization. That explanation should be attributed to their research rather than presented as an uncontested description of every border district. Their focus on workers’ experiences also matters: describing kolberi solely as an illicit flow of goods can conceal the household livelihood decisions and bodily risks involved. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "comparison-and-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Compare relationships, not stereotypes",
        "content": "These cases direct attention to who controls movement, who earns from it, and who carries its risks. Eastern transport history and Kurdish porter labor concern different periods and places. Neither establishes that border residents share one ethnicity, occupation, or relationship with government. A fuller account must add other frontiers and contemporary local evidence before making national comparisons. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Tribes and Smugglers in Iran's Eastern Borderlands, 1921–41",
        "publisher": "Cambridge University Press, Iranian Studies",
        "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/iranian-studies/article/tribes-and-smugglers-in-irans-eastern-borderlands-192141/DD4F635EB9E5DA317CBEB7348CE30459",
        "language": "English",
